## **Capitalization and AP title case**

We follow AP Style guidelines when capitalizing titles and subheadings. All H1s, H2s and H3s should utilize AP Title Case unless client notes specify otherwise.

- As part of AP Title Case, do capitalize:
    - The first word, last word and all nouns, pronouns, verbs, adverbs and adjectives.
    - Conjunctions and prepositions longer than three letters (this includes “From” and “With”).
    - *Is* and *It (Is* is a verb and *It* is a pronoun).
- Don’t capitalize: “a” “an” “and” “at” “but” “by” “for” “if” “in” “nor” “of” “on” “or” “so” “the” “to” “up” and “yet.”
    - *Exemption:* When a proper noun includes a word like “For”. For example, TikTok's "For You" page is an official name. DO capitalize "For" in this instance (although it is a preposition we would typically lowercase) because it is part of a proper noun.
    - **Note!** Some of these words may need to be capitalized based on their part of speech in a sentence. For example, if "so" or "yet" are used as an adverb (i.e. This Is So Awesome), capitalize them!
- **How to capitalize hyphenated words:** While there are no steadfast AP rules for hyphenated words and some style guides say to NOT capitalize words following first word in hyphen unless it’s proper (“Cost-effective” instead of “Cost-Effective”), **our house style is to capitalize the second word** i.e. How to Make a Well-**Informed** Decision About Siding.
    - DO capitalize the second word in a hyphened phrase *even if that word would typically be lowercase on its own*. Example: Built-**In** Feature
- **If subheadings are running long or in the form of a sentence:** This doesn’t happen often! As a rule of thumb, use AP title case for consistency with the rest of a project’s subheadings, unless the client notes specify otherwise.
    - If a subheading is running long, edit it to make it shorter if possible.

<aside>
‼️ **Differences between AP Title Case and our in-house style:**

1. The AP Stylebook says to capitalize “to” when using title case (i.e. question “How **T**o Bake a Cake”). However, our in-house style is to leave “to” lowercase (i.e. “How **t**o Bake a Cake”).

2. Some title case capitalization sites will capitalize preposition words in contexts like "Reach **O**ut" and "Sign **U**p." However, our in-house style is to leave these lowercase (i.e. "Reach **o**ut" and "Sign **u**p").

</aside>

Capitalize the first word after a colon in a sentence only if it is a proper noun or the start of a complete sentence.

– **Yes:** If you’re a webmaster then you know what we’re talking about: Spammers use referrer spam to draw attention.

– **No:** If you’re a webmaster then you know what we’re talking about: Referrer spam.

