## **Percentages**

Use the % symbol, with no space, instead of spelling out “percent.” Always use numerals to express percentages. If necessary, use decimals – not fractions – to express numbers less than 1%.

If fewer than 1%, use the numeral 0 before the decimal. (ex: 0.25%)

– **Yes:** 3.25%

– **No:** three and one-fourth percent

– **No:** 3.25 %

– **No:** 3.25 percent

Resources:

- AP Stylebook – percent, percentage, percentage points

## **Ranges and spans**

Use a hyphen (-) to indicate a range or span of numbers.

– It takes 20-30 days.

Note: If both numbers in a range are followed by “millions”, “%”, etc., use these expressions in both numbers in the range.

– **Yes:** $11 million-$21 million

– **No:** $11-$21 million

– **Yes:** 10%-12%

– **No:** 10-12%

Resources:

- AP Stylebook – ranges
- How to Write About Ranges

## **Money**

Always use numerals for monetary units, even if the number is less than 10. When writing about US currency, use the dollar sign before the amount. Include a decimal and number of cents if more than 0.

– $2

– $19.99

When writing about other currencies, follow the same symbol-amount format:

– ¥1

– €1

## **Telephone numbers**

Use dashes without spaces between numbers. Use a country code if your reader is in another country.

– 555-867-5309

– +1-404-123-4567

If a project has specific instructions to add a call link in the CTA, write out the number and create the link by inserting "tel:" in front of the phone number.

!phonenumber.png

## **Temperature**

Use numerals for all numbers except zero.

Use the degree symbol — you can copy-paste it from this site: https://www.degreesymbol.net/ — DO NOT use the word “degree”

Spell out the word “Fahrenheit” 

Spell out the word “minus”

Examples:

- 98**°** Fahrenheit
- The overnight low was minus 10º Fahrenheit.

*Note that our in-house directives here differ from AP Style.*

## **Time**

Use numerals and “a.m.” or “p.m.” with a space in between. Don’t use minutes for on-the-hour time. Separate hours from minutes with a colon (without a space in between).

– Yes: 7 a.m.

– Yes: 7:30 p.m.

– No: 7 AM

Use a hyphen between times to indicate a time period.

– 7 a.m.-10:30 p.m.

Specify time zones when writing about an event or something else people would need to schedule. We default to EST.

Abbreviate decades when referring to those within the past 100 years.

– the ’00s

– the ’90s

When referring to decades more than 100 years ago, be more specific:

– the 1900s

– the 1890s

