## **Colon**

The only time a colon is necessary is to introduce a bulleted list:

!example1.png

Or to set off an introductory phrase from the main part of a bulleted list:

!example2.png

Do not use colons to offset a separate thought — use an em dash instead.

– **No:** He could only think about one thing all day: the cookies waiting for him in the cookie jar at home.

– **Yes:** He could only think about one thing all day — the cookies waiting for him in the cookie jar at home.

## **Commas**

When writing a list, don’t use the serial comma (also known as the Oxford comma — the comma before the “and”).

– **Yes:** I like singing off-key, eating the last Oreo and finger painting.

– **No:** I like singing off-key, eating the last Oreo, and finger painting.

Use a comma before “but” only when “but” is connecting two independent clauses.

– I would quit my job, but I love my job so much.

The AP ruling on commas for short introductory phrases is a bit vague. A sentence with a long (more than four words) introductory clause would definitely need one: “When he had tired of the mad pace of New York, he moved to Dubuque.” However, the comma can be omitted after short phrases if no ambiguity would result: “During the night he heard many noises.” Based on this, words like **But** and **Finally** are short introductory phrases, so the comma isn’t needed!

